.TH NETD 4
.SH NAME
netd \- the network server: TCP/IP, DHCP, DNS (port "net")
.SH SYNOPSIS
Port
.BR net .
.B /bin/netd
is started on demand by
.BR init (8)
(the first lookup of "net"), stopped after 10 minutes unused with no
connection open.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B netd
does IPv4, ARP, ICMP (ping), UDP and TCP, and the DHCP and DNS clients.
It uses one interface at a time: the cable
.RB ( vnet (4),
port "nic0") when its link is up, else Wi-Fi
.RB ( wifid (4),
port "wifi") when it is associated, else the cable anyway (virtio has no
link to watch).
A change of interface is a new network: ARP forgotten, DHCP again.
.PP
One thread does everything, one message at a time: a client's request, a
batch of frames received, or a tick of the timer thread.
Calls that must wait keep the client's reply token and are answered later.
TCP measures the round trip, does slow start, congestion avoidance, fast
retransmit and window scaling, and keeps out-of-order segments.
.PP
A handle belongs to the process that opened it.
Addresses are numbers: a.b.c.d = a << 24 | b << 16 | c << 8 | d.
Calls that wait take a timeout in milliseconds (0: 30 s; NET_RECV: 0 =
forever).
.SH MESSAGES
.TP
.B NET_SOCKET
w[1] = NET_TCP or NET_UDP \-> handle.
.TP
.B NET_CONNECT
(TCP) w[1] = handle, w[2] = address, w[3] = port | timeout << 16 \-> 0.
.TP
.B NET_LISTEN
(TCP) w[1] = handle, w[2] = port (below 1024: root), w[3] = backlog \-> 0.
.TP
.B NET_ACCEPT
(TCP) w[1] = handle, w[3] = timeout \-> a new handle; reply w[1], w[2] = the
peer's address and port.
.TP
.BR NET_SEND ", " NET_RECV
w[1] = handle; sbuf = data (waits for room) \-> bytes; or w[2] = most bytes,
w[3] = timeout, rbuf <\- data \-> bytes, 0: the other side closed.
.TP
.BR NET_BIND ", " NET_SENDTO ", " NET_RECVFROM
(UDP) a local port (0: any); a datagram to w[2] = address, w[3] = port; one
datagram in rbuf, reply w[1], w[2] = the sender.
.TP
.B NET_CLOSE
w[1] = handle \-> 0.
.TP
.B NET_RESOLVE
sbuf = a host name, w[3] = timeout \-> 0; reply w[1] = the address.
.TP
.B NET_PING
w[1] = address, w[2] = time to live (0: 64), w[3] = timeout \-> the round
trip in microseconds; reply w[1] = who answered (with a small TTL, a router
on the way).
.TP
.BR NET_INFO ", " NET_SOCKETS
rbuf <\- net_info_t (address, mask, gateway, DNS from DHCP; MAC, MTU, the
time, counters, the interface and its link); net_sock_t[], every socket.
.TP
.B NET_POLL
w[1] = handle, w[2] = NET_IN | NET_OUT (0: either) \-> what it is ready for
now; if nothing it waits for, the caller is noted
.RB ( sys_note (2))
once it is.
.TP
.BR NET_FRAMES ", " NET_LINK
(from a network driver) frames received, each a 16-bit length then the
frame; the link came up or went down.
.SH ERRORS
\-ENETUNREACH, \-ECONNREFUSED, \-ECONNRESET, \-ETIMEDOUT, \-EHOSTUNREACH,
\-EADDRINUSE, \-ENOTCONN.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fetch (1),
.BR ping (1),
.BR vnet (4),
.BR wifid (4)
.SH BUGS
No IPv6, no fragments, no selective acknowledgements, no keepalives; routing
is "the local network or the gateway".
